WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.680
If a neuron survives, does it innervate its target normally?

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.820
Go back to the mice lacking both Bax and NGF.

3
00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.400
Their sympathetic neurons survive.

4
00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:16.173
Now look at their targets at birth.

5
00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:21.423
Innervation is missing from some targets, such as the salivary glands.

6
00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:25.267
It is greatly reduced in others, such as the heart.

7
00:00:26.107 --> 00:00:28.287
A few, such as the trachea, are near normal.

8
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.000
So survival does not mean normal innervation.

9
00:00:33.083 --> 00:00:36.603
Within a target, NGF helps axons grow and branch.

10
00:00:37.633 --> 00:00:42.533
So the factor that decides who lives also helps decide how much target each survivor

11
00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:43.533
covers.

12
00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:46.293
The broader task is called systems matching.

13
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:52.573
Each target gets a fitting number of neurons, amount of innervation, synapses and

14
00:00:52.573 --> 00:00:55.713
dendrite shapes. NGF acts on several.

15
00:00:56.667 --> 00:00:59.327
Now a different system, and a different variable.

16
00:01:00.347 --> 00:01:02.347
Chick motor neurons, and muscle activity.

17
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.680
Curare blocks the acetylcholine receptors of muscle.

18
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.540
The muscle stops responding to its nerve.

19
00:01:10.533 --> 00:01:15.853
Robert Pittman and Ronald Oppenheim gave curare to chick embryos during the period

20
00:01:15.853 --> 00:01:17.173
of motor neuron death.

21
00:01:18.117 --> 00:01:23.137
If active connections help an axon win, what should paralysis do to motor neuron

22
00:01:23.137 --> 00:01:24.137
death?

23
00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:31.410
You might expect more death.

24
00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:33.930
Instead, fewer motor neurons died.

25
00:01:34.933 --> 00:01:36.473
That looked like a contradiction.

26
00:01:37.573 --> 00:01:39.273
Silencing the connection saved neurons.

27
00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:44.267
The usual explanations are interpretations, so hold them loosely.

28
00:01:45.317 --> 00:01:50.157
The most common reading is that paralysed muscle may allow more nerve branching and

29
00:01:50.157 --> 00:01:51.157
more synapses.

30
00:01:51.917 --> 00:01:55.337
That would give each motor neuron more access to trophic support.

31
00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:59.720
Note that chick motor neurons do not depend on NGF.

32
00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.640
They draw on several factors at once.

33
00:02:03.583 --> 00:02:06.123
In other systems, activity helps a winner.

34
00:02:07.243 --> 00:02:09.563
Active synapses can release more BDNF.

35
00:02:10.633 --> 00:02:14.853
Activity in the muscle and activity in the competing neurons are different

36
00:02:14.853 --> 00:02:17.593
variables. The results need not conflict.

37
00:02:18.633 --> 00:02:20.413
So here is the rule once more.

38
00:02:21.253 --> 00:02:22.393
The target sets how many.

39
00:02:23.193 --> 00:02:24.433
The neurons decide which.

40
00:02:25.417 --> 00:02:31.057
In the published model, a lead grows because NGF raises TrkA and lengthens its

41
00:02:31.057 --> 00:02:32.057
signal.

42
00:02:32.367 --> 00:02:35.787
Punishment through p75 makes the losers die sooner.

43
00:02:36.817 --> 00:02:39.337
The rule is scoped to mouse sympathetic neurons.

44
00:02:40.377 --> 00:02:41.997
Chick motor neurons need their own account.

45
00:02:43.017 --> 00:02:49.557
Takeaway. NGF supply sets how many sympathetic neurons survive, the neurons' own

46
00:02:49.557 --> 00:02:54.657
feedback decides which, and NGF also shapes how much of many targets they cover.

47
00:02:55.837 --> 00:02:58.757
Muscle activity is a different variable, in a different system.
